course: dictionary
lesson: 7312
title: Placard: a sign you carry
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A placard is a large card or board with a message written on it. People carry placards at a protest or march, or put them up in public so everyone can read them. Context helps: in «The marchers raised their placards and chanted outside the town hall», the clues are marchers, raised and chanted, so a placard must be something held up with words on it.
explain: Watch out, because placard looks like a word you might know from French. «Le goûter est dans le placard» means "The snack is in the cupboard," because in French placard means cupboard. In English, a placard is never a cupboard. Check by swapping your meaning into the sentence: a snack in a sign would make no sense.
explain: When you meet a new word, ask what it does in the sentence. A placard is held, carried or put up, so it is something you can see and read. Skip any unknown words you are unsure of, read on, and then come back to them.
fact: A placard is a large card or board with a message written on it.
fact: Placard is a noun: a placard, placards.
fact: People carry placards at a protest or march, or put them up in public.
fact: In French, «placard» means cupboard, so it is a false friend: a word that looks the same in two languages but means something different.
fact: Suffragettes and marchers carried placards with slogans, and schools make them for climate strikes or charity events.
exercise: «The marchers raised their placards high.» What is a placard? | A small cupboard in the kitchen | *A large card with a message on it | A flag with only a picture | A loud whistle
exercise: «Le goûter est dans le placard.» In French, placard means what? | A happy song | *A cupboard | A card with a message | A protest march
exercise: Where would you most likely see placards? | In a swimming pool | Under a bed | Inside a fridge | *At a protest or march
test: «The class made placards for the charity walk.» What were the placards for? | Drawing a map of the school | Keeping the snacks cold | *A message for the charity walk | Hiding a secret cupboard
test: Ella says, «Le placard is where the cups are.» Why is Ella right, and why would an English speaker be confused? | Placard means cupboard in English too | *In French placard means cupboard, but in English it means a card or board with a message | Placard is a type of shoe | Placard means a loud protest song
exam: «The protesters held placards that read "Save Our Park".» What is a placard, and what does the French word placard mean? | *A large card with a message on it; in French it means cupboard | A protest; in French it means a message | A flag; in French it means a snack | A small cupboard in English; in French it means a card
